bibit-agenthub-plugin (0.3.14) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Change the catalog tag filter from a union to an intersection: a version
    matches only when it carries every selected tag, so selecting more tags
    narrows the list instead of widening it. Previously two selected tags
    returned everything tagged with either one.
  * The change is one predicate in `buildPowersResult` (`src/api/apm.ts`), which
    both scopes share, so installed and market behave identically. Tags stay
    per-version and the filter still runs on exact versions before the series
    grouping: of a series whose v2 carries both selected tags and whose v1
    carries only one, the series stays and only the matching version does.
  * No protocol change: `powers.list.v1` still projects `tags` as a sorted,
    unique, at most 20 entries list, the wire envelope is untouched and the
    Provider is untouched. Facets keep describing the whole type, so the tag
    buttons do not disappear as the selection narrows the list.
  * An empty selection still means "no tag filter", and unselecting back to
    empty restores the full list, so the new rule only ever applies from the
    second selected tag onwards.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 14:40:00 +0800


bibit-agenthub-plugin (0.3.13) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Turn the Settings tab's single APC credential into a saved-key list. The
    form-wide "Save settings" action and the "Clear saved key" workaround are
    gone: a key is typed and saved with its own "Save key" action, and it then
    appears in the list as a masked preview (`head****tail`) so the page can
    show which credentials exist without ever holding one.
  * Every saved key carries its own Enable and Delete action. Delete is a single
    click with no confirmation step — the row's button names the key it removes,
    unlike the catalog uninstall/delete flows which still confirm. Exactly one
    key may be enabled, and that key is the credential `apm submit` receives.
  * The enabled flag is decided by the provider, not the page. When the list is
    empty the first saved key is enabled; every later key arrives disabled, so
    saving a new key never silently switches the credential an upload is using.
    Deleting the enabled key leaves the remaining keys disabled until the user
    enables one, which is the explicit choice we settled on.
  * The settings protocol moves from one `apiKey` field to a key list:
    `settings.get.v2` returns `{keys:[{id,preview,enabled}]}` and three writes
    replace `settings.update.v1` — `settings.keys.add.v1` (the only operation
    that receives a credential, so the Registry `credentialParameters` entry
    moves there), `settings.keys.delete.v1` and `settings.keys.enable.v1`. The
    page learns a key only as a masked preview plus an opaque 128-bit id.
  * `settings.json` becomes schemaVersion 2 (`apiKeys: [{id,key,enabled}]`). A v1
    file is migrated on read: its single key becomes the one enabled entry, and
    the next write lands the v2 shape, so an existing installation keeps its
    credential. The file keeps its 0600 permissions and atomic replace, and a v2
    file that still carries `apiKey`, or one with two enabled rows, is refused
    rather than silently repaired.
  * The provider still owns validation end to end: printable-ASCII keys only,
    duplicates and unknown ids rejected as `incompatible`, at most 50 keys, and
    the masked preview is generated provider-side. The page re-validates the
    closed DTO and additionally refuses a preview without a mask, so a provider
    bug cannot put a credential on screen.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 11:05:00 +0800

