bibit-agenthub-plugin (0.3.9) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Give every Power card one fixed width, so a windowed card is never narrower
    than a fullscreen one, and only the column count follows the window. The grid
    used a hard-coded five columns of `1fr` plus breakpoints at 1240/980/720/480px,
    which let the track absorb the window width: with the host's own 15rem sidebar
    in the box, the card was 232.8px at a 1600px window, 200.8px at 1440px, 170px
    at 1276px and 159.5px at 1024px, against 276px fullscreen. The grid now holds a
    fixed `--hub-card-width` with `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(...))` and centres the
    track block, so the count still falls 5/4/3/2/1 while the card keeps its size.
  * Size that fixed card from BiBit's own default window rather than from the page
    column's 1460px cap. At 1460px five cards of 276px do fit, but the default
    window (1200x800, src-tauri/tauri.linux.conf.json) leaves the page only
    1200 - 240px sidebar - 2px shell = 958px, i.e. 862px of content column after
    the padding, so three 276px cards plus two gaps (868px) missed by 6px and the
    first window opened with two cards and 144px of empty space on each side. The
    width is now derived from the host geometry tokens (`--hub-default-window`,
    `--hub-host-sidebar`, `--hub-host-frame`, `--hub-page-padding`,
    `--hub-card-gap`) and comes out at 267.33px: three cards fit with ~20px to
    spare, and five still fit at the 1460px column cap.
  * Make the version slot one height on both card kinds. A Hub-managed card renders
    a native select (31px at 14px/1.5) while a hand-installed card renders a static
    label (26px), so the two headers differed by 5px and the custom card's title and
    description sat on a different line than its neighbours; the at-narrow-width
    card also pushed the Custom badge and the version slot past the card's content
    box by up to 36px. The static label now fills the same 31px slot and clips an
    over-long version inside it, and every card carries an explicit
    `grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr)` so an over-long version cannot widen it.
  * The layout contract test now resolves the card-width `calc()` against the
    declared tokens and fails if the host default window would no longer show
    exactly three columns, so the width cannot drift back on its own.
  * `docs/verification/probe-fixed-card-grid.py` renders the real page inside the
    host box (window - sidebar - shell border) and the real card markup offscreen in
    WebKit2 to measure the widths, the column counts and the header alignment;
    `--only shipped` asserts them, including the three columns at the default
    window.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:30:00 +0800

bibit-agenthub-plugin (0.3.8) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Read block scalar frontmatter and stop warning about Powers the APM
    manifest already manages. Two real Skills on the board (k3-media-pipeline,
    vpu-benchmark) write their description as a folded block scalar
    (`description: >-`), whose value lives on the following indented lines. The
    custom-resource scanner only understood inline scalars, so it read an empty
    description and rejected both directories as invalid-authority-file, which
    the page then reported as "does not meet the minimum format". The scanner now
    parses `|` and `>` headers (with their chomping/indentation indicators), folds
    `>` into space-joined lines with a newline per paragraph, keeps `|` literal,
    and lets sibling keys after the block survive; the Dev Host scanner mirrors
    it so both agree on what is listed. Separately, a skip is no longer reported
    as a warning when the APM manifest already manages that (type,name): the card
    for such a Power is fed by the manifest, so re-scanning its directory only
    ever produced noise about a Power that is listed correctly.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 14:55:00 +0800

bibit-agenthub-plugin (0.3.7) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Confirm an uninstall before running it, and return to the Hub list once it
    is confirmed gone. Uninstalling is destructive, so the dialog footer now
    arms an in-dialog confirmation naming agent/type/name, exactly like the
    custom-resource delete; Cancel backs out without a request. After the write
    reconciles, the page closes the dialog when the Power is confirmed removed
    -- including the case where apm reported a non-zero exit but the reconciled
    list already shows the Power gone, since the state the user asked for is
    true and staying on the dialog would hide the reinstall entry point. A failed
    uninstall, a still-installed record, or a reconcile that could not run all
    keep the dialog open with its reason. Installing is unchanged: it still
    submits on one click and keeps its dialog.

